\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSFileWrapper holds a file\'27s contents in dynamic memory. In this role it enables a document object to embed a file, treating it as a unit of data that can be displayed as an image (and possibly edited in place), saved to disk, or transmitted to another application. It can also store an icon for representing the file in a document or in a dragging operation.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Instances of this class are referred to as \i file wrapper objects\i0 , and when no confusion will result, merely as \i file wrappers\i0 . A file wrapper can be one of three specific types: a \i regular file wrapper\i0 , which holds the contents of a single actual file; a \i directory wrapper\i0 , which holds a directory and all of the files or directories within it; or a \i link wrapper\i0 , which simply represents a symbolic link in the file system (sometimes called a shortcut or alias). Some NSFileWrapper methods apply only to a specific type, and raise an exception if sent to a file wrapper of the wrong type. To determine the type of a file wrapper, use the \b isRegularFile\b0 , \b isDirectory\b0 , and \b isSymbolicLink\b0  methods.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You can create a file wrapper from data in memory using \b initWithSerializedRepresentation:\b0  or from data on disk using \b initWithPath:\b0 . Both create the appropriate type of file wrapper based on the nature of the serialized representation or of the file on disk. Three convenience methods each create a file wrapper of a specific type: \b initRegularFileWithContents:\b0 , \b initDirectoryWithFileWrappers:\b0 , and \b initSymbolicLinkWithDestination:\b0 . Because each initialization method creates file wrappers of different types or states, they\'27re all designated initializers for this class\'d0subclasses must meaningfully override them all as necessary.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Memory and Disk Representations\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Because the purpose of a file wrapper is to represent files in memory, it\'27s very loosely coupled to any disk representation. A file wrapper doesn\'27t record the path to the disk representation of its contents. This allows you to save the same file wrapper with different paths, but it also requires you to record those paths if you want to update the file wrapper from disk later. NSFileWrapper allows you to set a preferred filename for save operations and records the last filename it was actually saved to; the \b preferredFilename\b0  and \b filename\b0  methods return these names. This feature is more important for directory wrappers, though, and so is discussed below under \'aaWorking with Directory Wrappers.\'ba\
\fs14\
\fs28 A file wrapper stores file system information (such as modification time and access permissions), which it updates when reading from disk and uses when writing files to disk. The \b fileAttributes\b0  method returns this information in the format described in the NSFileManager class specification. You can also set the file attributes using the \b setFileAttributes:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 When saving a file wrapper to disk, you typically determine the directory you want to save it in, then append the preferred filename to that directory path and use \b writeToFile:atomically:updateFilenames:\b0 , which saves the file wrapper\'27s contents and updates the file attributes. You can save a file wrapper under a different name if you wish, but this may result in the recorded filename differing from the preferred filename, depending on how you invoke the \b writeToFile:...\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Besides saving its contents to disk, a file wrapper can re-read them from disk when necessary. The \b needsToBeUpdatedFromPath:\b0  method determines whether a disk representation may have changed, based on the file attributes stored the last time the file was read or written. If the file wrapper\'27s modification time or access permissions are different from those of the file on disk, this method returns YES. You can then use \b updateFromPath:\b0  to re-read the file from disk.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Finally, to transmit a file wrapper to another process or system (for example, over a distributed objects connection or through the pasteboard), you use the \b serializedRepresentation\b0  method to get an NSData object containing the file wrapper\'27s contents in the NSFileContentsPboardType format. You can safely transmit this representation over whatever channel you desire. The recipient of the representation can then reconstitute the file wrapper using the \b initWithSerializedRepresentation:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Working with Directory Wrappers\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 A directory wrapper contains other file wrappers (of any type), and allows you to access them by keys derived from their preferred filenames. You can add any type of file wrapper to a directory wrapper with \b addFileWrapper:\b0  or \b addFileWithPath:\b0 , and remove it with \b removeFileWrapper:\b0 . The convenience methods \b addRegularFileWithContents:preferredFilename:\b0  and \b addSymbolicLinkWithDestination:preferredFilename:\b0  allow you to add regular file and link wrappers while also setting their preferred names.\
\fs14\
\fs28 A directory wrapper stores its contents in an NSDictionary, which you can retrieve using the \b fileWrappers\b0  method. The keys of this dictionary are based on the preferred filenames of each file wrapper contained in the directory wrapper. There exist, then, three identifiers for a file wrapper within a directory wrapper:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab Preferred filename. This doesn\'27t uniquely identify the file wrapper, but the following identifiers are always based on it.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab Dictionary key. This is always equal to the preferred name when there are no other file wrappers of the same preferred name in the same directory wrapper. Otherwise, it\'27s a string made by adding a unique prefix to the preferred filename (note that the same file wrapper can have a different dictionary key for each directory wrapper that contains it). You use the dictionary key to retrieve the file wrapper object in memory, in order to get its contents or its filename (to update it from disk). You can get a file wrapper\'27s dictionary key by sending a \b keyForFileWrapper:\b0  message to the directory wrapper that contains it.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab Filename. This is usually based on the preferred filename, but isn\'27t necessarily the same as it or the dictionary key. You use the filename to update a single file wrapper relative to the path of the directory wrapper that contains it. Note that the filename may change whenever you save a directory wrapper containing the file wrapper (particularly if the file wrapper has been added to several different directory wrappers); thus, you should always retrieve the filename from the file wrapper itself each time you need it rather than caching it.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 When working with the contents of a directory wrapper, you can use a dictionary enumerator to retrieve each file wrapper and perform whatever operation you need. Note that with the exceptions of saving and updating, a directory file wrapper defines no recursive operations for its contents. To set the file attributes for all contained file wrappers, or to perform any other such operation, you must define a recursive method that examines the type of each file wrapper and invokes itself anew for any directory wrapper it encounters.\
\fs14\
